<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Origen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 185–c. 253</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Origen was a Christian theologian and scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and one of the most productive writers of the early church. He taught in Alexandria and later at Caesarea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, compiled the Hexapla, a comparison of versions of the Old Testament, and wrote commentaries and the treatise On First Principles. He was tortured in the persecution of Decius and died a few years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/origen.jpg" alt="Origen" width="220" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Origen3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
